It seems like the issue is related to the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header being set to `\*`, which allows requests from any origin. When you try to include an authorization header, it might be getting ignored or overridden by the wildcard value.

To resolve this issue, you can try setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to a specific domain or IP address that you trust. For example, if your application is running on `http://localhost:3000`, you can set the header to `http://localhost:3000`.

Here's an example of how you can do this in Node.js using the `express` framework:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use(function (req, res, next) {
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", "http://localhost:3000");
  next();
});

app.listen(3000);
```
Alternatively, you can use a more flexible approach and set the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to a list of allowed origins. For example:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use(function (req, res, next) {
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", ["http://localhost:3000", "http://example.com"]);
  next();
});

app.listen(3000);
```
In this example, the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header will allow requests from both `http://localhost:3000` and `http://example.com`.

