To configure the boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a manifest file (manifest.json) for your Chrome extension. This file will contain information about your extension, such as its name, version, and permissions. Here's an example of a basic manifest file:
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Inject JavaScript",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Inserts JavaScript code into an existing webpage.",
  "permissions": ["activeTab"]
}
```
1. Create a background script (background.js) for your extension. This script will handle the injection of JavaScript code into the webpage. Here's an example of a simple background script:
```javascript
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
  if (request.action === 'inject-javascript') {
    chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, function(tabs) {
      chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: 'inject.js'});
    });
  }
});
```
1. Create an inject.js file for your extension. This file will contain the JavaScript code that you want to inject into the webpage. Here's an example of a simple inject.js file:
```javascript
console.log('JavaScript injected!');
```
1. Package your extension by creating a folder with the following structure:
```
extension/
  - manifest.json
  - background.js
  - inject.js
```
1. Install your extension by going to `chrome://extensions/` in your Chrome browser, enabling "Developer mode" (top right corner), and clicking "Load unpacked" to select the folder you created in step 4.
1. To test your extension, open a webpage in Chrome and click the extension icon in the toolbar. Select "Inject JavaScript" from the dropdown menu to inject the JavaScript code into the webpage.

