To create a React utility that removes widows, you can use the `useEffect` hook and the `getComputedStyle` method to check if any elements have a width of less than 100%. If an element is found with a width less than 100%, you can adjust its width to prevent it from becoming a widow. Here's an example:

```javascript
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = () => {
  useEffect(() => {
    const handleResize = () => {
      const elements = document.querySelectorAll('[data-remove-widows]');
      elements.forEach((element) => {
        const style = getComputedStyle(element);
        const width = parseFloat(style.width);

        if (width < 100) {
          element.style.width = '100%';
        }
      });
    };

    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div data-remove-widows>
      {/* Your content here */}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

In this example, we use the `useEffect` hook to listen for the `resize` event. When the event is triggered, we select all elements with the `data-remove-widows` attribute and check their width. If the width is less than 100%, we set the width to 100% to prevent it from becoming a widow.

You can use this component in your React application by importing it and wrapping your content with it:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <RemoveWidows>
      {/* Your content here */}
    </RemoveWidows>
  );
};

export default App;
```

This will ensure that any elements with a width less than 100% are adjusted to prevent them from becoming widows.

To create a React utility that removes widows, you can use the `useEffect` hook and the `getComputedStyle` method to check if an element has a widow. If it does, you can adjust its width to avoid the widow. Here's a simple example:

```javascript
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ children }) => {
  useEffect(() => {
    const elements = document.querySelectorAll('.your-class');

    elements.forEach((element) => {
      const style = window.getComputedStyle(element);
      const width = style.width;
      const padding = style.paddingLeft + style.paddingRight;

      if (width - padding < element.offsetWidth) {
        element.style.width = `${width - padding}px`;
      }
    });
  }, [children]);

  return <div className="your-class">{children}</div>;
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

Replace `.your-class` with the actual class or selector of the elements you want to apply this utility to. You can then use this component in your React application like this:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello, World!</h1>
      <p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.</p>
      <RemoveWidows>
        <p>This is a long paragraph that may cause a widow.</p>
      </RemoveWidows>
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

This utility will automatically adjust the width of elements with the specified class or selector to avoid widows. Keep in mind that this is a basic example and may not work perfectly for all cases. You may need to adjust the logic based on your specific requirements.

